    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: Redesign owner identity to `<REGION>-<HASH8>`\n\nThe `customerCode` is now a deterministic region-and-hash identity with the sequence numbers dropped entirely. Everything derived from the customer code follows automatically.\n\n### Files changed\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Replaced the old `nextCustomerCode(city, lastName)` (which built `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` from a per-city sequence) with `customerCode(owner)`:\n- `REGION` = `Region.code(postcode, city)` \u2014 the canonical region resolver (postcode-preferred, city fallback, `UNKNOWN` otherwise).\n- `HASH8` = first 8 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedTelephone + lastName`, reusing the existing `hashPrefix` primitive.\n- The per-city `ownersInCity` count is now used only by the capacity check; its javadoc was updated.\n- The **audit record** already logs `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so it now emits the new identity (verified in the log: `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`).\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Moved the derived values onto the new identity:\n- **`locality`** now reads the `REGION` segment out of the `customerCode` (falling back to `Region.code` only when no code is present, e.g. seed data) instead of recomputing from postcode/city.\n- **`membershipNumber`** (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`) and **`checkDigit`** (Luhn over the code's digits) already read `customerCode`, so they now build on the new identity; only their javadocs/examples were updated.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Updated the `customerCode`, `membershipNumber`, and `locality` descriptions and examples to document the `<REGION>-<HASH8>` format.\n\n### Verification\n- `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n- The acceptance test `coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash` (asserts `customerCode` matches `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}`) passes, and the audit log confirms the end-to-end format (`NSW-16E6609E`).\n\nOne interpretation worth flagging: I resolved `REGION` via the shared `Region.code(postcode, city)` (postcode-preferred with city fallback) rather than postcode-only. This keeps region resolution in the codebase's single canonical place and preserves locality values for owners identified by city, while still satisfying \"derived from the postcode\" (postcode takes precedence). If you want it to be *strictly* postcode-only (so a city-only owner gets `UNKNOWN`), that's a one-line change to `Region.forPostcode(...)`.",
